<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amu Darya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oxus, now called the Amu Darya, is a major river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> flowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. It has long marked the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. Its valley was central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>, and it marked a frontier for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxus.jpg" alt="Oxus" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amudarya_by_Nukus,_Uzbekistan_(%D0%90%D0%BC%D1%83%D0%B4%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8C%D1%8F_%D1%80%D1%8F%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B0_%D0%9D%D1%83%D0%BA%D1%83%D1%81).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
